   {
    "ruling": "M177",
    "headwords": [
     "sovereign-origin-error",
     "origin-sovereignty-error"
    ],
    "why": "The ruling changes meaning rather than repairing it. The audit says S400's invariant must be repaired 'to match its common meaning: a creator claiming ownership'. But S400's common meaning, example ('only self-made minds count') and invariant all describe demanding self-origin, which is the same concept as O413. Applying the ruling means rewriting the whole entry into a new concept (creator ownership), which overlaps origin-not-owner and origin-capture. Decision needed: alias S400 to O413, or rewrite S400 as a new concept."
   },
   {
    "ruling": "M136",
    "headwords": [
     "grief-support"
    ],
    "why": "The ruling strips [DIG] 'unless online infrastructure is part of the invariant'. Domain lines record where a word applies, not its invariant; online grief communities are a real application. Held for a decision rather than applied."
   },
   {
    "ruling": "M121",
    "headwords": [
     "just-refusal",
     "grounded-refusal",
     "justified-refusal"
    ],
    "why": "The audit's repair names no-with-reason and refuse-with-reason, which are not this row. The real row's entries already state that Law I needs no reason. The 'transparent, not justified' point argues for keeping grounded-refusal distinct from justified-refusal; which pair to alias waits for Luna's machine-readable row."
   },
   {
    "ruling": "M040-M044 and all ALIAS/CONSOLIDATE rows",
    "headwords": [],
    "why": "Alias metadata is applied from Luna's review JSONL with the audit's overrides (phase2/alias_overrides.json). It cannot be applied until that file is in the workbench."
   }
  ]
